# Ore's reduction of Shafarevich's theorem to split embedding problems

This directory (`Submission/Work/Fitting/`, namespace `Work.Fitting`) builds the finite group
theory used in Ore's (1939) deduction of Shafarevich's theorem from the solvability of *split*
embedding problems with finite nilpotent kernel.  The classical proof (Shafarevich 1954;
Schmidt–Wingberg, arXiv:math/9809211, §5 "Proof of Šafarevič's theorem"; Huppert, *Endliche
Gruppen I*, III.3.2(b) and III.4.2(c)) runs as follows.  Let `G ≠ 1` be finite and solvable,
`F = F(G)` its Fitting subgroup (the largest normal nilpotent subgroup) and `Φ(G)` its Frattini
subgroup.

* `Φ(G) ≤ F(G)`, and in fact `Φ(G) < F(G)` for `G ≠ 1` solvable (`frattini_lt_fitting`,
  Schmidt–Wingberg Proposition 17, Huppert III.4.2(c));
* hence `F(G) ⊄ Φ(G)`, so `F(G)` has a *proper supplement* `U < G` with `F(G)·U = G`
  (`exists_supplement_of_not_le_frattini`, Schmidt–Wingberg Proposition 16, Huppert III.3.2(b));
* `U` is a proper subgroup, so by induction on `|G|` it is realizable as a Galois group over `ℚ`,
  say `U ≅ Gal(L/ℚ)` for a finite Galois `L/ℚ` inside `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`;
* conjugation makes `F(G)` a `U`-module (`ψ`); transporting along `U ≅ Gal(L/ℚ)` gives an action
  `φ : Gal(L/ℚ) →* MulAut F(G)`, and the split embedding problem
  `1 → F(G) → F(G) ⋊[φ] Gal(L/ℚ) → Gal(L/ℚ) → 1` is solvable by hypothesis;
* `F(G) ⋊[ψ] U ≅ F(G) ⋊[φ] Gal(L/ℚ)` and `(x,u) ↦ x·u : F(G) ⋊[ψ] U → G` is surjective, so
  `G`, being a quotient of a realizable group, is realizable (`RealizableOver.of_surjective`).

The whole deduction is `Work.Fitting.realizable_of_solvable_of_split_embedding`
(`Submission/Work/Fitting/SplitEmbedding.lean`); the only hypothesis is that every split
embedding problem over `ℚ` with finite nilpotent kernel is solvable (Theorem 14 of
Schmidt–Wingberg for the base field `ℚ`).

## Contents

* `Submission/Work/Fitting/Basic.lean` : the definition `Work.Fitting.fitting G` of the Fitting
  subgroup (the supremum of all normal nilpotent subgroups), with `le_fitting`, `fitting_le_iff`,
  `fitting_normal`, `fitting_characteristic`, `fitting_eq_top`, `frattini_le_fitting`,
  `frattini_ne_top`, `fitting_ne_bot`, `exists_minimal_normal`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/PCore.lean` : the `p`-core `Work.Fitting.pCore p G` (the largest normal
  `p`-subgroup), `le_pCore`, `pCore_normal`, `pCore_isPGroup`, `pCore_nilpotent`,
  `pCore_le_fitting`, and `Subgroup.map_subtype_normal_of_characteristic`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/FrattiniAux.lean` : `le_frattini_iff`, `exists_coatom_ge`,
  `isCoatomic_subgroup`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/iSup_sylow_eq_top.lean` : a finite nilpotent group is generated by its
  Sylow subgroups.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/isNilpotent_iSup_of_normal_pgroups.lean` : the join of finitely many
  normal `p`-subgroups attached to pairwise distinct primes is nilpotent.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/FittingEqPCore.lean` : `le_iSup_pCore_of_isNilpotent_normal`,
  `fitting_eq_iSup_pCore`, and **`fitting_nilpotent`** : `F(G)` is nilpotent, i.e. `F(G)` is the
  *largest* normal nilpotent subgroup.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/FrattiniFitting.lean` : `le_fitting_of_isPGroup_comap`, the preimage
  in `G` of a normal `p`-subgroup of `G ⧸ M` lies in `F(G)` when `M ≤ Φ(G)`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/isNilpotent_of_isNilpotent_map_quotient.lean` : if `M ≤ Φ(G)` and
  `H/M` is nilpotent then `H` is nilpotent (the Frattini–Fitting lemma).
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/sup_sylow_eq_top_of_isPGroup_quotient.lean` : if `M ◁ K` and `K/M` is a
  `p`-group, then `K = M ⊔ Q` for every Sylow `p`-subgroup `Q` of `K`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/FittingQuotient.lean` : `frattini_map_le` and
  **`fitting_map_quotient`** : `F(G/M) = F(G)M/M` for `M ≤ Φ(G)`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/frattini_map_quotient.lean` : `Φ(G/M) = Φ(G)M/M` for `M ≤ Φ(G)`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/FrattiniLtFitting.lean` : `card_quotient_lt` and
  **`frattini_lt_fitting`** : `Φ(G) < F(G)` for nontrivial finite solvable `G`.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/exists_supplement_of_not_le_frattini.lean` : the supplement lemma.
* `Submission/Work/Fitting/SplitEmbedding.lean` : the reduction.  `SplitEmbeddingProblem L N φ` is
  the embedding problem `1 → N → N ⋊[φ] Gal(L/ℚ) → Gal(L/ℚ) → 1` (an instance of
  `Submission.EmbeddingProblem ℚ` with projection `SemidirectProduct.rightHom`), and there are
  three forms of the reduction theorem:
  - **`realizable_of_solvable_of_split_embedding`** : the requested form, with the hypothesis
    `∀ L N φ, (SplitEmbeddingProblem L N φ).IsSolvable` (here `G : Type` and `N : Type`, since
    `Submission.EmbeddingProblem` lives in universe `0`);
  - **`realizable_of_solvable_of_split_solvable`** : universe-polymorphic, with the hypothesis
    `∀ L N φ, SplitEmbeddingSolvable L N φ`, the *unfolded* form of "the split embedding problem
    has a proper solution" (`SplitEmbeddingSolvable` is to `EmbeddingProblem.IsSolvable` what the
    latter is for the SplitEmbeddingProblem, but without the universe restriction);
  - **`realizable_of_solvable_of_split_embedding_univ`** : universe-polymorphic, with the weaker
    hypothesis `∀ L N φ, RealizableOver ℚ (N ⋊[φ] Gal(L/ℚ))`.

  A caller whose group is not in universe `0` should use one of the last two (a hypothesis stated
  through `Submission.EmbeddingProblem` cannot be instantiated at other universes).
-/
